      "context": "Some Thessalonian believers had died, and the church feared that those Christians might somehow be disadvantaged when Jesus returned. Paul’s answer is pastoral: death does not cause believers to miss the Lord’s coming. Those who have died in Christ will rise, living believers will join them, and the whole community will be with the Lord. The passage ends with a command to encourage one another, revealing its primary purpose.\n\nThe language of being “caught up” and going “to meet” the Lord has played a major role in later Christian discussions about the rapture. Christian traditions differ over whether this passage describes a distinct removal of the church before a tribulation, the public arrival of Christ with His people going out to welcome Him, or another relationship between these images and other end-time texts. The Greek term for “meeting” can be used for going out to welcome an arriving dignitary, but that observation alone does not settle every eschatological question. This paragraph does not specify a tribulation timetable, millennium sequence, or date.",
