      "context": "Paul responds to questions about “times and seasons” by saying the church already knows the day of the Lord comes like a thief. His answer refuses a schedule and redirects attention to identity and conduct. “Peace and security” may echo ordinary human confidence and may also have resonances with Roman imperial language, but interpreters should not claim more certainty than the evidence allows. The main contrast is between false complacency and sober readiness.\n\nThe second half of the chapter shows what readiness looks like inside a congregation. Leaders are respected but not made unquestionable. Different people receive different care. The Greek word translated “idle,” “disorderly,” or “undisciplined” can refer to people out of step with community responsibility; it should not be flattened into a judgment against everyone without paid work. The commands to rejoice, pray, give thanks, receive prophecy, test everything, and abstain from evil all require context and wisdom rather than slogans.",
